      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To better understand the sequence, first consider Prophase, where chromosomes condense and become visible; this helps students grasp the organization. Next, in Metaphase, the chromosomes line up neatly in the middle of the cell, which is a key point to learn. Anaphase follows, guiding the separated chromosomes to opposite ends, so students can clearly see the division occurring. Finally, Telophase completes the process as two new nuclei form, allowing students to understand how two identical cells are made.",
      "source": "target",
